---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import AgendaSection from '../components/AgendaSection.astro';

const schedule = [
  { start: '10:00 AM', end: '10:05 AM', min: 5, topic: 'Introduction', anchor: 'introduction' },
  { start: '10:05 AM', end: '10:10 AM', min: 5, topic: 'Set the Stage', anchor: 'set-the-stage' },
  { start: '10:10 AM', end: '10:15 AM', min: 5, topic: 'Share Your Back Story', anchor: 'back-story' },
  { start: '10:15 AM', end: '10:20 AM', min: 5, topic: "Share the Attendee's Journey", anchor: 'attendees-journey' },
  { start: '10:20 AM', end: '10:32 AM', min: 12, topic: 'Breakout Rooms', anchor: 'breakout-rooms' },
  { start: '10:32 AM', end: '10:40 AM', min: 8, topic: 'Breakout Room Debrief', anchor: 'breakout-debrief' },
  { start: '10:40 AM', end: '10:41 AM', min: 1, topic: 'Exercise: Amplification Goal', anchor: 'amplification-goal' },
  { start: '10:41 AM', end: '10:56 AM', min: 15, topic: 'Exercise: Three Futures', anchor: 'three-futures' },
  { start: '10:56 AM', end: '11:20 AM', min: 24, topic: 'Exercise: Identify, Interact, Influence Model', anchor: 'iii-model' },
  { start: '11:20 AM', end: '11:30 AM', min: 10, topic: 'Ah-Ha! Moment Share', anchor: 'aha-1' },
  { start: '11:30 AM', end: '11:33 AM', min: 3, topic: 'Quick Break', anchor: 'break' },
  { start: '11:33 AM', end: '11:35 AM', min: 2, topic: 'Exercise: Self-Assessment Review', anchor: 'self-assessment' },
  { start: '11:35 AM', end: '11:40 AM', min: 5, topic: 'Share and Tell', anchor: 'share-and-tell' },
  { start: '11:40 AM', end: '12:03 PM', min: 23, topic: 'Second Teaching Moment: The Membership + Method', anchor: 'second-teaching' },
  { start: '12:03 PM', end: '12:06 PM', min: 3, topic: 'Ah-Ha Moment Share', anchor: 'aha-2' },
  { start: '12:06 PM', end: '12:36 PM', min: 30, topic: 'The Pitch: The Membership', anchor: 'pitch' },
  { start: '12:36 PM', end: '12:51 PM', min: 15, topic: 'Wrap Up Teaching Moment', anchor: 'wrap-up' },
  { start: '12:51 PM', end: '12:55 PM', min: 4, topic: "Last Aha's / Comments", anchor: 'last-ahas' },
  { start: '12:55 PM', end: '1:00 PM', min: 5, topic: 'Closing Story', anchor: 'closing' },
];
---

<Layout
  title="Event Agenda — Marketing Workshop | Debugging Human Communication"
  description="Facilitator agenda for the Debugging Human Communication marketing workshop."
  noindex={true}
>
  <main class="bg-teal-50 min-h-screen">
    <!-- Header -->
    <section class="bg-teal-950 px-6 py-10 md:py-14">
      <div class="container-lg">
        <p class="text-teal-300 text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider mb-2">Facilitator Agenda</p>
        <h1 class="heading-hero !text-white mb-4">Marketing Workshop — Triangle Model</h1>
        <p class="text-teal-200 text-lg md:text-xl max-w-3xl">
          <strong class="text-white">Runs 10:00 AM – 1:00 PM (3 hours).</strong> Click any section to expand. Use the controls below to expand or collapse everything.
        </p>

        <div class="mt-6 grid md:grid-cols-3 gap-4 text-sm">
          <div class="bg-teal-900/60 border border-teal-800 rounded-lg p-4">
            <p class="text-teal-300 text-xs uppercase font-semibold tracking-wider mb-1">Offer pitched</p>
            <p class="text-teal-100">Debugging Human Communication Membership — $5,000 PIF six months → $500/month continuation.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-teal-900/60 border border-teal-800 rounded-lg p-4">
            <p class="text-teal-300 text-xs uppercase font-semibold tracking-wider mb-1">Audience</p>
            <p class="text-teal-100">Analytical professionals with thought-leadership ambitions — engineers, CTOs, entrepreneurs, agency founders, technical consultants.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-teal-900/60 border border-teal-800 rounded-lg p-4">
            <p class="text-teal-300 text-xs uppercase font-semibold tracking-wider mb-1">Core transformation</p>
            <p class="text-teal-100 italic">How would your life change if you could consistently get the right message to the right person?</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Schedule + Controls -->
    <section class="bg-white border-b border-teal-200 sticky top-0 z-40 shadow-sm">
      <div class="container-lg px-6 py-4">
        <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-3 mb-4">
          <button type="button" id="expand-all" class="btn-primary-sm">Expand all</button>
          <button type="button" id="collapse-all" class="inline-block bg-teal-200 text-teal-950 font-semibold px-5 py-2.5 rounded-lg hover:bg-teal-300 transition-colors">Collapse all</button>
          <details class="ml-auto">
            <summary class="cursor-pointer text-teal-800 hover:text-teal-950 font-medium text-sm">Jump to section ▾</summary>
            <div class="absolute right-6 mt-2 bg-white border border-teal-200 rounded-lg shadow-lg p-3 max-w-md z-50 max-h-96 overflow-y-auto">
              <ul class="text-sm space-y-1">
                {schedule.map((s) => (
                  <li>
                    <a href={`#${s.anchor}`} class="block px-2 py-1 rounded hover:bg-teal-50 text-teal-900">
                      <span class="font-mono text-xs text-teal-700 mr-2">{s.start}</span>
                      {s.topic}
                    </a>
                  </li>
                ))}
              </ul>
            </div>
          </details>
        </div>

        <details class="bg-teal-50 rounded-lg border border-teal-200">
          <summary class="cursor-pointer px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-teal-950 select-none">Full schedule</summary>
          <div class="px-4 pb-4 overflow-x-auto">
            <table class="w-full text-sm">
              <thead>
                <tr class="text-left text-teal-700 border-b border-teal-200">
                  <th class="py-2 pr-4 font-semibold">Start</th>
                  <th class="py-2 pr-4 font-semibold">End</th>
                  <th class="py-2 pr-4 font-semibold">Min</th>
                  <th class="py-2 font-semibold">Topic</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                {schedule.map((s) => (
                  <tr class="border-b border-teal-100 last:border-0">
                    <td class="py-2 pr-4 font-mono text-teal-800">{s.start}</td>
                    <td class="py-2 pr-4 font-mono text-teal-800">{s.end}</td>
                    <td class="py-2 pr-4 text-teal-700">{s.min}</td>
                    <td class="py-2"><a href={`#${s.anchor}`} class="text-teal-900 hover:text-coral underline decoration-teal-300">{s.topic}</a></td>
                  </tr>
                ))}
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Sections -->
    <section class="py-10 px-6">
      <div class="container-lg">

        <AgendaSection id="introduction" title="Introduction" timing="5 minutes — end by 10:05 AM">
          <h3>Welcome & Participant Introduction</h3>
          <p>Greet everyone by name as they join. Invite each participant to introduce themselves.</p>
          <blockquote>"Hey [Name], great to have you with us! Go ahead and share where you're joining from, what you work on, and what you're building or speaking about publicly right now."</blockquote>
        </AgendaSection>

        <AgendaSection id="set-the-stage" title="Set the Stage" timing="5 minutes — end by 10:10 AM">
          <h3>This is a working laboratory</h3>
          <blockquote>"This isn't a lecture and it's not a pep talk. For the next three hours, <strong>think of this as a laboratory</strong> — a place where you experiment on your own communication system, try new things, and walk out with something you didn't have when you walked in. Real clarity on <strong>how to get the right message to the right person, every time</strong>."</blockquote>

          <h3>How to get the most out of this</h3>
          <blockquote>"<strong>Give yourself permission to really think</strong> today — no distractions, no multitasking. This is your time to go deep the way you love to. Take notes, share in the chat, whatever helps you get the most out of our time together."</blockquote>

          <h3>What you'll leave with</h3>
          <blockquote>"By the end of today, you'll have a clear picture of <strong>where your communication has the most untapped leverage</strong> — and your <strong>first experiment to run this week</strong>. Not theory. Not a reading list. Something you can try in a real conversation and see what changes."</blockquote>
        </AgendaSection>

        <AgendaSection id="back-story" title="Share Your Back Story" timing="5 minutes — end by 10:15 AM">
          <h3>The Origin</h3>
          <blockquote>"I want to tell you how I got here. I got my degree in marketing, and I spent the first part of my career in business communication and sales. So the communication piece is what you'd expect. The part you might not expect is how I ended up doing this work with engineers.<br /><br />I reconnected with my friend <strong>Scott at our high school reunion</strong>. He'd been building a software company and he asked me to come on as CEO. I was confused — I said, 'Scott, I'm not a programmer.' And he said, 'You may not think you're a programmer, but you definitely think like one.'<br /><br />So we co-founded Corgibytes together. We fell in love, got married, had kids. And here's what I kept noticing: Scott was one of the most compassionate people I'd ever met. But he kept saying he was 'bad with people.'"</blockquote>

          <h3>The Discovery</h3>
          <blockquote>"That didn't add up to me. So I started digging. I saw communication the way he saw code — as a system with patterns, rules, and logic. And when I got into the research on human communication, the overlap was enormous.<br /><br />Once we started treating communication as a system and not a personal flaw... emotions as data and not a distraction... interactions as tiny experiments instead of personal failings — everything unlocked. Scott didn't just get 'better with people' — he became the person other people cite. He speaks. He's referenced. His ideas move through the industry."</blockquote>

          <h3>The Pattern</h3>
          <blockquote>"Over 15 years, I kept seeing the same thing — in engineers, in entrepreneurs, in founders, in technical leaders. Smart people with important ideas — ideas that their industries <em>need</em> — and those ideas weren't getting the traction they deserved. Not because the ideas were bad. Because the communication system around the ideas wasn't optimized for scale."</blockquote>

          <h3>The Bridge</h3>
          <blockquote>"That's why I do this work. <strong>I'm a person who understands business, but I also understand you.</strong> I speak your language. I've built this work for people whose ideas deserve to be heard by bigger rooms than they're currently reaching."</blockquote>
        </AgendaSection>

        <AgendaSection id="attendees-journey" title="Share the Attendee's Journey" timing="5 minutes — end by 10:20 AM">
          <p class="stage-direction">Narrate their journey back to them so they feel seen. This is for analytical professionals who want to scale their influence — not for people who feel broken.</p>

          <h4>1. Transition from your story to theirs</h4>
          <blockquote>"So that's my story — but here's what I know about you..."</blockquote>

          <h4>2. Reflect who they are</h4>
          <blockquote>"<strong>You're good at what you do.</strong> You've put in the years. You've built the systems, solved the hard problems, delivered when it mattered. People come to you when they need a real answer. Your expertise is not in question."</blockquote>

          <h4>3. The ambition</h4>
          <blockquote>"And now you want more than expertise. <strong>You want your ideas to turn into action</strong> — in whatever form moving looks like for you. Maybe it's getting the complex idea you've been trying to explain turned into something your organization actually acts on. Maybe it's closing the client, the deal, the opportunity you keep almost-landing. Maybe it's moving into the role you've been ready for — staff engineer, director, VP, founder. Maybe it's speaking at the conferences you've been attending, writing the book, or growing the consultancy. Maybe it's just being heard at the leadership table — not because you're the loudest, but because your thinking deserves to be there."</blockquote>

          <h4>4. The friction</h4>
          <blockquote>"But <strong>there's a gap between how good your work is and the impact it's having.</strong>
            <ul>
              <li>You <strong>create real business value</strong> in the technical details. Maybe you fixed a bug that prevented a huge outage. Or were able to make one tiny tweak to a process that ended up having an outsized impact, but when it comes time for performance reviews, <strong>none of it seems to be noticed.</strong></li>
              <li>You pay attention to the <strong>tiny details of how things will be implemented</strong>, taking the time to guard against every edge case, but then when you go to explain it to your client their <strong>eyes glaze over</strong>. You can tell you're overwhelming them and losing their interest.</li>
              <li>Or you have <strong>ideas that you know are industry changing</strong> and have ambitions of being on stages, writing books, and collaborating with the people you admire, but you just <strong>can't find the words that land</strong>. You over explain. The ideas that are crystal clear in your mind get jumbled when you try to speak or write.</li>
            </ul>
            You know you've got so much potential that is being held back because you haven't figured out the communication patterns that make everything click."</blockquote>

          <h4>5. Acknowledge their frustration</h4>
          <blockquote>"And the frustrating part is — <strong>you KNOW there's a system here.</strong> You can feel it. You can almost see the patterns, the rules, the structure — but every time you try to pin them down, they slip. <strong>You've tried to reverse-engineer</strong> it the way you'd reverse-engineer any other system. And it keeps not quite resolving. Not because you're not smart enough. Because the patterns are real, but the framework to make them visible isn't one you've encountered yet."</blockquote>

          <h4>6. Acknowledge their efforts</h4>
          <blockquote>"You've tried. <strong>You don't sit there and complain.</strong> You've put in the work — reading books, taking courses, watching talks. You've taken away some pretty decent tips and tricks, but <strong>haven't figured out how they all fit together.</strong> When you try to implement it, it doesn't quite click. You can't quite figure out how to adapt it to your specific situation. <strong>You're craving a system, not a collection of tactics.</strong> You want to know exactly what to do and what will work with confidence."</blockquote>

          <h4>7. Their current desire</h4>
          <blockquote>"Right now, <strong>you're ready to work on this the way you'd work on any hard technical problem.</strong> Form hypotheses. Run experiments. Measure results. Iterate. You don't need inspiration. You need a method."</blockquote>

          <h4>8. The good news</h4>
          <blockquote>"And here's the <strong>good news — that's exactly how we're going to work today.</strong> Not with checklists you'll never use. Not with generic frameworks. You're going to leave with a diagnostic of where your communication system has the most leverage — and a clear sense of what to experiment with next."</blockquote>
        </AgendaSection>

        <AgendaSection id="breakout-rooms" title="Breakout Rooms" timing="12 minutes — end by 10:32 AM">
          <h3>Instructions</h3>
          <blockquote>"We're going to do a quick breakout room so you can meet the other people in this session. First thing — assign a group leader. The leader's job is to set a timer and make sure everyone gets a turn."</blockquote>

          <h3>Prompt</h3>
          <blockquote>"<strong>Share your name, what you work on, and one place where your work deserves to be having a bigger impact than it currently is.</strong>"<br /><br />"It could be a complex idea you can't get people to act on, a plan you've proposed that leadership won't fund, a client you keep almost-closing, a promotion or role you're ready for but haven't been offered, a consultancy you're trying to grow, a talk or book or post you've been meaning to put out, an argument your organization needs but can't quite hear yet. Keep it specific."</blockquote>

          <p><strong>Time:</strong> 12 minutes total. ~3 minutes per person in groups of 4.</p>

          <h3>Send-off</h3>
          <blockquote>"<strong>This is your first chance to connect with people who get it — people who think like you do</strong>, at the stage you're at. I'll see you back here in just a few minutes."</blockquote>
        </AgendaSection>

        <AgendaSection id="breakout-debrief" title="Breakout Room Debrief" timing="8 minutes — end by 10:40 AM">
          <blockquote>"Welcome back! Who met someone great in their breakout room?"</blockquote>
          <p>Invite 3–4 people to share what they discussed — specifically, what they said they wanted to reach more people with.</p>
          <p>Acknowledge each share and connect across the room: <em>"Notice how many of you said something similar about [theme]. That's not a coincidence — that's a pattern in this niche."</em></p>
          <p><strong>Bridge:</strong> <em>"Now let's look at the framework that's going to show you exactly where in your communication system you're leaving impact on the table."</em></p>
        </AgendaSection>

        <AgendaSection id="amplification-goal" title="Exercise: Amplification Goal" timing="1 minute — end by 10:41 AM">
          <blockquote>"<strong>In the chat, type your answer to this question: <em>How would your life change if you could consistently get the right message to the right person?</em></strong>"<br /><br />"Maybe it's closing the client you've been chasing. Landing the keynote. Getting the budget approved. Securing the investor. Publishing the book. Finally being heard at the leadership table."</blockquote>
          <p>Read responses aloud, identify patterns.</p>
          <blockquote>"Sounds like the common theme in this room is [closing deals / getting buy-in / reaching bigger audiences / being recognized for what you actually know]. That's exactly what we're going to work on."</blockquote>
        </AgendaSection>

        <AgendaSection id="three-futures" title="Exercise: Three Futures" timing="15 minutes — end by 10:56 AM">

          <h3>Pitch Part 1 — Today and the Future</h3>

          <p class="stage-direction">Visual: a box labeled "today" on the left, "three years from now" on the right, three branches between them — green (best), yellow (plateau), red (failure). Whiteboard or animated slide.</p>

          <h4>Ask for permission to begin the exercise</h4>
          <blockquote>"Before I teach anything, I want to do an exercise with you — and this is the part where we start to dig deeper. So if you don't have something to write with, go ahead and grab a pen or open a document. I'm going to show you how to measure how your communication is impacting your goals and where you want to be so we can see what success looks like.Is it okay if I walk you through it?"</blockquote>

          <h4>Set up the visual</h4>
          <blockquote>"Okay. This is where you are today. Over here — three years from now. Between now and then, three things can happen — the best case, the okay case, and the failure case. The gap between them is where we're going to find the leverage for the amplification goal you just named."</blockquote>

          <h4>Best case — GREEN (amplifying)</h4>
          <blockquote>"If everything went your way over the next three years — your ideas landed, your work got seen, the right people paid attention — what would be true for you that isn't true today? Type it in the chat. Be specific: a role, a stage, a project, a client, a number."</blockquote>
          <ul>
            <li>You translate complex thinking into words that make people act — the direction you set gets followed, the plan you proposed gets funded, the decision you recommended gets made. No more re-explaining. No more "let's circle back."</li>
            <li>You're closing the clients, deals, and opportunities you used to almost-close — the right message hits the right person the first time.</li>
            <li>You've moved into the role you've been ready for — staff, director, VP, founder, leadership table — because the value of your work is finally visible.</li>
            <li>You're getting invited to speak, publish, or consult on work you actually care about — and saying yes or no on your terms.</li>
            <li>You have a community of other analytical minds who ship work into the world together.</li>
            <li>Your ideas are cited, referenced, built on by others — inside your org and across your field.</li>
          </ul>

          <h4>The plateau — YELLOW (plateaued)</h4>
          <blockquote>"Now the okay case. Not disaster — just not transformational. You kept the job. You kept the reputation. Nothing fell apart. But three years pass and nothing actually scaled. What does that version look like for you? Type it in the chat."</blockquote>
          <p class="stage-direction">Read 2–3 responses. Acknowledge how easy it is to land here by default — it's not failure, which is exactly what makes it the most common place capable people end up.</p>
          <ul>
            <li>You're respected for your expertise but your public presence has stalled.</li>
            <li>You get local wins but can't replicate them beyond your immediate team.</li>
            <li>You wrote the post, gave the talk, pitched the idea — and it did fine, not great.</li>
            <li>You're not sure why some of your ideas land and others disappear.</li>
            <li>You've read the books, watched the talks — you can't quite apply what you learned to your specific situation.</li>
          </ul>

          <h4>The failure case — RED (stuck below your level)</h4>
          <blockquote>"And the failure case. What does it look like three years from now if you can't get the right message to the right people? </blockquote>
          <p class="stage-direction">This prompt is harder to answer — give space. Read 2–3 if they come.</p>
          <ul>
            <li>You have the expertise but your reputation, revenue, and audience don't reflect it.</li>
            <li>You lose deals, opportunities, or budget — not because your thinking is wrong, but because your message didn't land.</li>
            <li>You know you're underselling what you do — clients, investors, or leadership don't understand the full value of what you're offering.</li>
            <li>The ideas that are crystal clear in your mind get jumbled when you try to explain them to someone who thinks differently than you.</li>
            <li>You've stopped proposing things because you can't tell what gets through and what doesn't.</li>
          </ul>

          <h4>Name the cost</h4>
          <blockquote>"Look at the space between green and red. That gap represents the difference of having messages that land and make an impact or continuously missing the mark. And this is cumulative, too. Communication compounds. It's not just one conversations, but hundreds, maybe thousands over the course of three years."</blockquote>
          <p class="stage-direction">Gesture physically between the green and red branches on the visual. Let the silence land for a beat before moving on.</p>

          <h4>Name the cause — why most people drift into red</h4>
          <blockquote>"Here's the thing — nobody plans to end up in the red. Nobody walks into their career thinking, 'I'll have great ideas and watch them never leave my head.' Everybody in this room wants to be up here in the green. But most capable people drift down into the red anyway. Why?"</blockquote>
          <blockquote>"Three reasons. <strong>No framework</strong> — they've never seen communication laid out as a system they can actually reason about. <strong>No community</strong> — they're trying to figure it out alone, without other analytical minds running the same experiments on themselves. <strong>No method</strong> — they have tips and tactics, but no experimental practice for how communication scales. Missing any one of these is enough to keep you stuck. Missing all three is why the drift happens by default."</blockquote>

          <h4>Ask for permission</h4>
          <p class="stage-direction"><strong>This is the explicit permission ask — the pivot from exercise into offer.</strong> Do not skip it. Do not rush it. Do not treat it as rhetorical. Ask the question, then stop talking. Wait for visible acknowledgments (chat replies, thumbs up, nods on camera) before moving into Pitch Part 2. Asking for permission — and actually receiving it — is what turns this from a pitch into an invited conversation. If the room goes quiet, prompt directly: <em>"Type 'yes' in the chat if you'd like me to walk you through it."</em></p>
          <blockquote>"What I want to do next is share the diagnostic tool I developed to help people move from red or yellow to green. We're going to walk through it systematically so you have time to figure out precisely where your communication gaps are.Is it okay if I walk you through what that looks like?"</blockquote>
        </AgendaSection>

        <AgendaSection id="iii-model" title="Exercise: Identify, Interact, Influence Model" timing="24 minutes — end by 11:20 AM">

          <h3>Pitch Part 2 — The Model (Self-Assessment)</h3>

          <blockquote>"Great. So the first thing we have to evaluate is how well we're able to <strong>identify where our communication gaps are.</strong> If we can't observe it, we can't improve it."</blockquote>
          <blockquote>"<strong>Step 1 is recognizing our locus of control.</strong> This means we take accountability for our communication outcomes. We stop blaming other people. We stop making excuses. We stop playing the victim. We recognize our responsibility and commit to actively improve our skills. This isn't about being perfect. There is no such thing in communication. You're going to mess up. Mistakes are going to happen. But the question is: what do you do about it when they do? Do you stay passive and hopeless? Or do you get curious so you can learn from your mistakes and try something different the next time?</blockquote>
          <blockquote>"For each of these milestones, I want you to rate yourself of where you are today. Be really honest with yourself. Red means that you're really struggling. You know this is a blocker that you have to resolve. Yellow means there's some friction and room for improvement. And green means you've got this part down!"</blockquote>

          <h3>Pillar 1: Identify — Find the Root Cause</h3>

          <h4>Locus of Control Shifted</h4>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Red:</strong> "When my ideas don't land, I often blame other people or the circumstances."</li>
            <li><strong>Yellow:</strong> "I know intellectually that I own my half of every interaction, but I still catch myself feeling hopeless instead of recognizing that I have agency."</li>
            <li><strong>Green:</strong> "I take full accountability for my communication outcomes. If my idea didn't land, I own that and I can iterate on it."</li>
          </ul>

          <h4>Communication System Mapped</h4>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Red:</strong> "Being a good communicator is something other people are born with. I'm just not a people person."</li>
            <li><strong>Yellow:</strong> "I know there are patterns but I can't figure out what they are."</li>
            <li><strong>Green:</strong> "I understand the scientific principles behind human communication and use models to pinpoint exactly where a communication breakdown happened in most interactions."</li>
          </ul>

           <p class="stage-direction">Shift to the Shannon-Weaver model to give a demonstration of a specific communication model backed by science.</p>

          <h4>Problem Decomposed (Pebble Problems)</h4>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Red:</strong> "When something goes wrong, it feels like one giant mistake that can't be solved."</li>
            <li><strong>Yellow:</strong> "I can identify broad areas (like 'I talk too much' or 'I lose people who think differently than me') but can't isolate the specific component that's failing."</li>
            <li><strong>Green:</strong> "I can break a communication problem into its smallest testable parts — word choice, timing, framing, audience."</li>
          </ul>

          <h3>Pillar 2: Interact — Test What Works</h3>

          <h4>Patterns Revealed</h4>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Red:</strong> "Every conversation feels different. I don't start interactions with a clear strategy."</li>
            <li><strong>Yellow:</strong> "I notice patterns after the fact in hindsight, but I can't use them in real-time."</li>
            <li><strong>Green:</strong> "I can identify the heuristics driving a conversation as it's happening — mine and theirs — and adjust my approach on the fly."</li>
          </ul>

          <h4>Audience Targeted</h4>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Red:</strong> "I talk the same way to everyone — my CEO, my team, my board, my customers. That's just who I am and they need to get used to it."</li>
            <li><strong>Yellow:</strong> "I know I should adapt to different audiences, but I don't know what different people need."</li>
            <li><strong>Green:</strong> "I can accurately assess what will land with different people and tailor my message so it lands for them specifically."</li>
          </ul>

          <h4>Hypothesis Tested</h4>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Red:</strong> "I'm just bumbling through conversation after conversation hoping it doesn't go sideways."</li>
            <li><strong>Yellow:</strong> "I try different approaches, but I can't tell what works and what doesn't."</li>
            <li><strong>Green:</strong> "I design key conversations as experiments. I define my predictions, isolate variables, and frame my observations so they're measurable. Then I iterate on what I've learned."</li>
          </ul>

          <h3>Pillar 3: Influence — Make It Scalable</h3>

          <h4>Clarity Refined</h4>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Red:</strong> "My ideas make sense to me, but when I try to explain them to other people, they don't get it."</li>
            <li><strong>Yellow:</strong> "There are some people who totally understand what I'm saying, but other people who just don't connect with what I'm saying at all."</li>
            <li><strong>Green:</strong> "I can generally explain complex concepts to any audience — executives, customers, investors, a stranger on a plane — in a way that lands."</li>
          </ul>

          <h4>Persuasion Sharpened</h4>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Red:</strong> "When I share my opinions, more often than not things don't go as planned."</li>
            <li><strong>Yellow:</strong> "I know I'm well liked, but I can't seem to get other people to act on my ideas. It feels like luck when it does work."</li>
            <li><strong>Green:</strong> "I can negotiate, navigate conflict, build coalitions, and steer outcomes reliably — not by being louder, but by understanding what moves people."</li>
          </ul>

          <h4>Impact Expanded</h4>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Red:</strong> "I feel completely invisible and ignored when I share my ideas."</li>
            <li><strong>Yellow:</strong> "I have influence in some circles, but wish my ideas were heard more broadly."</li>
            <li><strong>Green:</strong> "I'm the person whose ideas are heard, referenced, and acted on — inside my company, in my industry, on stages."</li>
          </ul>

          <blockquote><strong>Close:</strong> "Remember the question I asked earlier — how would your life change if you could consistently get the right message to the right person? These nine milestones are the system behind that. My goal is to help you turn all of these green — not through a curriculum you work through alone, but in a community of practice where we're all running experiments together."</blockquote>
        </AgendaSection>

        <AgendaSection id="aha-1" title="Ah-Ha! Moment Share" timing="10 minutes — end by 11:30 AM">
          <blockquote>"How many of you had some big aha moments during that self-assessment? Who wants to share?"</blockquote>
          <p>Invite verbal shares + chat engagement. Specifically ask: <em>"Which milestone surprised you — where your self-rating was more red, or more green, than you expected?"</em></p>
          <p>Affirm each share: <em>"That's powerful — and that's a specific, testable thing you can actually work on."</em></p>
          <p><strong>Bridge:</strong> <em>"Those are exactly the kind of observations that turn into your first experiments."</em></p>
        </AgendaSection>

        <AgendaSection id="break" title="Quick Break" timing="3 minutes — end by 11:33 AM">
          <blockquote>"Take a few minutes to stretch, grab water, reset. We'll be back at [time]."<br /><br />"While you're up, look at your self-assessment. Notice which milestone you marked reddest. That's your highest-leverage experiment."</blockquote>
        </AgendaSection>

        <AgendaSection id="self-assessment" title="Exercise: Self-Assessment Review" timing="2 minutes — end by 11:35 AM">
          <p>Have attendees tally their R/Y/G marks across all 9 milestones.</p>
          <p><strong>Prompt:</strong> <em>"Write down your three reddest milestones. That's where you want to start developing your experiments. A small move on a red is worth more than a big move on something you're already yellow or green on."</em></p>
        </AgendaSection>

        <AgendaSection id="share-and-tell" title="Share and Tell" timing="5 minutes — end by 11:40 AM">
          <p>Call on people to share their biggest gap areas.</p>
          <p><strong>Normalize:</strong> <em>"Nobody in this room is green on everything — and you shouldn't be. Anyone claiming green across all 9 would be overstating. And this changes based on the situation, too. When things are uncertain, new, or we're stressed — all of that impacts our ability to communicate. The point is to know <strong>specifically</strong> where you are, so you know specifically where to experiment."</em></p>
          <p><strong>Bridge:</strong>But here's the challenge — experimenting alone can be really hard. One person said that when she tried, it felt like performing heart surgery on herself. That's because you're so close to the problem. So that's why I created the Debugging Human Communication community. Is it okay with you if I share what I've designed?"</p>
        </AgendaSection>

        <AgendaSection id="second-teaching" title="Second Teaching Moment: The Membership + Method" timing="23 minutes — end by 12:03 PM">

          <h3>Pitch Part 3 — The Method</h3>

          <blockquote>"Here's the core insight that shapes how we work together: you don't learn communication by reading books or sitting in a classroom listening to a lecture. You learn it the same way you've learned everything that matters in your career — by running experiments, measuring what happens, and iterating."</blockquote>

          <blockquote>"Debugging Human Communication is a community of practice, not a curriculum. Everything we do is built on the scientific method, because it's the surest way to discover what works."</blockquote>

                    <blockquote>"First, we have<strong>Labs</strong> twice a week. Wednesdays from 11am - noon and Thursdays from 3-4 eastern. I facilitate most of these, but I also have lab assistants who facilitate when I'm on vacation, get sick, or am giving a talk or a corporate training. Everyone brings a pebble problem, we vote on which ones we want to focus on as a group, and we walk away with specific experiments that we can test. It's really empowering because you're around people who are working on similar problems and are there to help you when you get stuck. Experiments are at the heart of everything and the lab sessions are where the magic happens."</blockquote>

          <blockquote>"Next, we have <strong>Thought Experiment Workshops</strong> — This is where we answer the "How do I..." questions by exploring the underlying science of human communication. Some workshops that are coming up are: How do I advocate for quality work when everyone else is focused on speed? How do I make messages simple without losing precision? How do I disagree without coming across as difficult."</blockquote>

          <h3>Pitch Part 4 — Small Wins That Compound</h3>

          <blockquote>"The membership isn't a curriculum you march through. It's a system where you can have immediate wins that built over time. Look back at the first exercise we did. This is how you get to the green and not the red. Not by passively ingesting more information, but by taking an active role and experimenting in community."</blockquote>

          <div class="callout-note"><strong>[Client stories: Soumya getting headcount, Lada getting paid to speak, Ellis overcoming his introversion, etc.]</strong></div>
        </AgendaSection>

        <AgendaSection id="aha-2" title="Ah-Ha Moment + Experiment Share" timing="3 minutes — end by 12:06 PM">
          <blockquote>"What was one insight or shift in perspective that really hit home for you about this method?"</blockquote>
          <p>Quick shares from 2–3 people + chat engagement.</p>
          <blockquote>"You're right — that's why this is such a powerful approach and why I'm building this community. I'd like to share a bit more about how it's set up. Is everyone okay with that?"</blockquote>
        </AgendaSection>

        <AgendaSection id="pitch" title="The Pitch: The Membership" timing="30 minutes — end by 12:36 PM">

          <h3>Pitch Part 5 — What's Included</h3>

          <blockquote>"Here's what membership in the community includes:"</blockquote>

          <ul>
            <li><strong>Onboarding Session</strong> - when you join, you'll start with a 90-minute goal-setting workshop with me. I want to make sure that you're really clear on what you're working towards because knowing what success looks like is essential to running successful experiments.</li>
            <li><strong>Online Community</strong> — you'll also be able to connect with other members whenever you need to. This is where we can all stay in touch and celebrate our wins, swap experiments, ask for support, and build relationships. Members can host their own events. We've done things like book clubs and deliberate practice groups. Prepping for a hard conversation, stuck drafting a post, debriefing a meeting that just happened? The community is there between sessions.</li>
            <li><strong>Labs</strong> — Like I said before, labs are where the magic happens. It's the steady rythym where good intentions turn into actionable experiments that you can test immediately. We meet twice a week: Wednesdays from 11am - noon and Thursdays from 3-4pm eastern. I attend as many as I can, but when I'm not able to because I'm sick, or on vacation, or have a corporate training I'm running, there are lab assistants who will step in and facilitate. The meetings run like this: the first few minutes are spent getting clear on what problem you want to solve. Then, everyone shares and we vote on which ones we want to focus on. We take about 8-10 minutes for a discussion and then the person who submitted the problem defines a specific experiment they're going to test. We usually get through 3-4 experiments per session and I keep track of who has gone so we can ensure everyone gets a chance to participate. And when there's a big breakthrough — you've got a group of people who are excited to celebrate with you.</li>
            <li><strong>Thought Experiment Workshops</strong> — This is where I share what the science says. While I have over 20 years of experience as a professional communicator, it's the research, not my intuition that drives what I share. You can't get this content anywhere else. I custom develop these workshops based on the challenges that members are grappling with and I make them interactive, just like this workshop has been, and I design experiment templates that go with each topic to make it as easy for you to start making progress as possible. Sessions take place every other Monday from 4pm - 5:30pm eastern. </li>
            <li><strong>Library</strong> — every Thought Experiment is recorded and added to a members-only library, along with additional resources and materials. If you can't attend live or want to revisit a pattern, the archive is there. </li>

          </ul>

          <blockquote>"What's NOT in here, on purpose: there's no 20-hour course to slog through. There's no certificate. There's no pre-planned curriculum to march through. This is a working practice built around real experiments that address real challenges from real people like you."</blockquote>

          <h3>Pitch Part 6 — Pricing</h3>

          <blockquote>"The membership is <strong>$5,000 for six months. After that, it's $500 per month as long as you want to continue</strong>."</blockquote>

          <p class="stage-direction">Let that land. Pause.</p>

          <blockquote>"Here's what I want you to compare that to. Think about what changes when you have access to high-quality experiments that help you address the challenges you've already identified. You've already asked yourself what would change if you could consistently get the right message to the right person. Take a moment and consider the impact this community can have on your situation."</blockquote>

          <p><strong>And since we like data, let's make this concrete with some numbers:</strong></p>
          <ul>
            <li>How much more would you earn from a promotion, raise, or bonus. $10K? $20? $100K+?</li>
            <li>Maybe you're interested in public speaking. Clients I work with have gone from speaking for free to charging $5,000+ per engagement.</li>
            <li>If you own a business, how much more would you earn from closing more deals and landing better clients? $50K? $100K? $500K+?</li>
            <li>It might be an investor conversation. If the right message hit the right person the first time: that could be the difference between funded and "interesting, keep us posted"</li>
          </ul>

          <blockquote>"Compare six months of membership in a community where you're not just sitting in a classroom — you're experimenting in the real world, and you've got the support you need to keep going when things get hard and the expertise to design experiments based on real research instead of guesswork. You could spend $5000 on a certification program or about a dozen sessions of executive coaching, but is that giving you the most value to help you get from where you are today to what you've defined as success?"<br /><br />"If even one of the outcomes we talked about today happens — the promotion, the talk, the project that gets funded — the membership pays for itself many times over."</blockquote>

          <div class="callout-note">
            <p><strong>Internal notes (not for stage):</strong></p>
            <ul>
              <li>Stated from stage: $5,000 for first 6 months, then $500/month to continue.</li>
              <li>Payment plan: NONE — PIF only. Do not offer installment plans.</li>
              <li>Scholarship floor (feedback call only, never stated from stage): $3,000. Requires explicit acknowledgment.</li>
              <li>Secret tiers (Founder $225/mo, Experimenter $0) are for the named pre-April-24 list only — not relevant for this event's prospects.</li>
              <li>The goal-setting 1:1 is a one-time onboarding session at the start, not ongoing 1:1 coaching.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <h3>Pitch Part 7 — The Feedback Call</h3>

          <blockquote>"You can't buy anything today. I'm not going to share any links for you to sign up because I want today to be about value. What I will share is a link to my calendar. I want to hear about your specific situation, understand what you're trying to amplify, and help you figure out if this is the right fit right now. If it is, great — I'll walk you through how to get started. If it's not, I'll tell you that too, and I'll point you toward what might work better."<br /><br />"Even if you already know this isn't the right season, I'd still love to get your feedback on today's session. That's a huge gift as I get this project off the ground."</blockquote>

          <div class="callout-note"><strong>Note:</strong> "You can't buy anything today" framing works well — reduces pressure, increases trust with technical audience. Every paying member so far has come through a feedback call, not a stage pitch. Feedback calls are already scheduled per the funnel gate — remind attendees of theirs; don't create a new one on-stage.</div>

          <p><strong>CTA:</strong> Remind attendees of their scheduled feedback call.</p>
        </AgendaSection>

        <AgendaSection id="wrap-up" title="Wrap Up Teaching Moment" timing="15 minutes — end by 12:51 PM">
          <h3>Closing Thoughts — Your First Experiment</h3>

          <blockquote>"Before we wrap up, I want to leave you with something you can use immediately — not homework, not a checklist. Just one small experiment you can run this week.<br /><br />Look at your self-assessment. Find the milestone you marked reddest, or the one that surprised you. Pick one — not all three.<br /><br />Now think of one real conversation, meeting, or piece of content you have coming up in the next seven days. Not a made-up scenario — a specific, scheduled thing.<br /><br />Form one hypothesis. 'If I try [specific thing], then I expect [specific outcome] to happen.' Write it down. Then run it. Then notice what actually happened.<br /><br />That's the whole practice. One observation. One hypothesis. One experiment. One debrief.<br /><br />Now that you know the system, you're on your way to mastering the hidden patterns of human communication. You can do it on your own and you'll see movement. If you do it with proprietary frameworks, a community of people like you running experiments alongside you, and a coach helping you design them — that's the membership. That's the compounding version.<br /><br />Either way: run one experiment, this week. You don't redesign the whole system on day one. You observe one thing. You watch what happens. You gather data.<br /><br />Communication works the same way. The skills you already have as a problem-solver — observe, hypothesize, test, iterate — are exactly the skills this practice is built on."</blockquote>
        </AgendaSection>

        <AgendaSection id="last-ahas" title="Last Aha's / Comments" timing="4 minutes — end by 12:55 PM">
          <blockquote>"Before we close, I'd love to hear any last takeaways or comments — especially: what's the one experiment you're going to run this week?"</blockquote>
          <p>Invite unmuting or chat. Acknowledge each share warmly. When someone names a specific experiment, reinforce it: <em>"That's a great one. Write it down. Run it. Let me know what happens."</em></p>
        </AgendaSection>

        <AgendaSection id="closing" title="Closing Story" timing="5 minutes — end by 1:00 PM">
          <h3>Closing Story — You're Already Doing This</h3>

          <blockquote>"I want to leave you with something that might shift how you see yourself.<br /><br />Think about what you do every day. When you simplify a complex system so the next person can understand it. When you write documentation that saves someone else hours of confusion. When you build a process that works so well people don't even notice it's there. When you take the time to improve something — not because you have to, but because you know someone else will be impacted by it.<br /><br />Who is that for? Not the computer. Machines don't care.<br /><br />You're doing it for people. You've always been doing it for people.<br /><br />That's communication. It's analyzing the human systems that you're a part of and recognize that you can make it better. It's a clear process. A clean handoff. A decision that aged well because you thought about who it would affect.<br /><br />Regardless of what your triangle looks like, I'm here to tell you that you are not as far away from your next level as you think you are.<br /><br />The thinking is already there. The dedication is already there. The care for the people — that's already there. Debugging Human Communication was designed for you so you can translate what you already do into a practice that scales into impact — to executives, to investors, to customers, to conference audiences, to the broader world that needs to hear what you're thinking.<br /><br />That's what this membership is built for. That's what the feedback call is for.<br /><br />Thank you for being here today. I can't wait to hear about the experiment you run this week."</blockquote>
        </AgendaSection>

      </div>
